# Line-ending discipline for the coding-governance chain.
#
# DELIBERATELY ADDITIVE: this file pins only paths the governance adoption INTRODUCES. There is no
# `* text=auto` and no `*.ps1` rule, because swydee's 28 pre-existing tracked blobs are already LF
# in the index (verified with `git cat-file -p HEAD:<path> | cat -A`) while the working tree is
# CRLF via core.autocrlf=true. A repo-wide rule would be a no-op at best and would renormalize
# every tracked file in one commit at worst. Pin what is new; leave the existing corpus alone.

# Execution-sensitive scripts — these run under git-bash. A stray CR breaks the shebang silently.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Git hooks are extensionless, so the *.sh rule above misses them. A CRLF hook does not execute,
# which would leave every pre-commit gate dormant while still looking installed.
.githooks/pre-commit text eol=lf

# Bash-sourced kit config. memory-tree sources this directly, so a trailing CR would make
# MEMORY_ROOT literally "memory<CR>" and every path built from it would miss.
.memory-tree.conf text eol=lf

# The gate leg manifest is runtime-read by run-gates.sh; a stray CR would corrupt leg names/argv.
tools/gate-legs.json text eol=lf

# memory-tree determinism: hygiene check 9 regenerates TREE.md and diffs it against the committed
# copy, so a CRLF checkout would fail the check for a reason that has nothing to do with the tree.
memory/**/TREE.md text eol=lf
memory/project/legacy-files.txt text eol=lf
memory/project/curation-debt.txt text eol=lf

# The rendered recall Skill is compared to a fresh render with plain `diff` by
# `adopt-memory-recall.sh --check`. The renderer always emits LF, so an unpinned CRLF checkout
# reds the leg on every line — while passing whenever the working copy happens to have just been
# rendered. That is green-by-accident, not a gate.
.claude/skills/memory-recall/SKILL.md text eol=lf
